UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 1: Using space, props and costumes', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIn a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show pride, and the bear''s low and back position shows shyness.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station. Draw a stage plan showing where actors stand, add two props and one costume piece, and write the reason for each choice. Perform the scene using things you already have at home and keep walkways clear.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station. Draw a stage plan showing where actors stand, add two props and one costume piece, and write the reason for each choice. Perform the scene using things you alre Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show… Reasoning: I used the stage space by attending to a prop rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots… Correction: Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The… Why: the correction restores accurate use of the stage space.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating the stage space on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with the stage space, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The… This matches the lesson skill for the stage space.","options":["Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The…","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"In a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have. Which response is most accurate?