The…","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about the stage space."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a prop?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The…","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Choose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station. Draw a stage plan showing where actors stand, add two props and one costume piece, and…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating the stage space on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show… Reasoning: I used the stage space by attending to a prop rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with the stage space, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots… Correction: Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The… Why: the correction restores accurate use of the stage space.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w14-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise the stage space.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use the stage space with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a prop and explain why it matters.","I can practise a costume independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a level needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a…","options":["Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIn a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show pride, and the bear''s low and back position shows shyness.","explain":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Level and space on stage.","caption":"Place actors with purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Level</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">king on a box, servant kneels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lion front, bear back</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience know Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience know Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A prop and a costume.","caption":"Objects tell the story.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Prop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">torch in a dark cave</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Costume</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">paper crown for a king</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Here is a more helpful example: Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show pride, and the bear''s low and back position shows shyness.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDrama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 1: Using space, props and costumes","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Role, character and storytelling","week":14,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Level and space on stage.","caption":"Place actors with purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Level</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">king on a box, servant kneels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lion front, bear back</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A prop and a costume.","caption":"Objects tell the story.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Prop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">torch in a dark cave</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Costume</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">paper crown for a king</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w14-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
